WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.460 --> 00:00:06.320
[fizzling]

00:00:07.680 --> 00:00:13.040
I was talking to one of my students, and mentioned how years ago,

00:00:13.260 --> 00:00:21.680
one of my coworkers had put a Coke can in a lab chiller, and it had blown up,

00:00:22.100 --> 00:00:25.260
and the bits had damaged the pump of the chiller.

00:00:26.260 --> 00:00:32.960
And then I suddenly thought, what would happen if we put a Coke can in liquid nitrogen?

00:00:34.080 --> 00:00:38.200
So late last night, I went out and bought two cans of coke.

00:00:38.360 --> 00:00:44.680
This was a first for me - I've never bought cans of coke, and I've never tasted any.

00:00:45.000 --> 00:00:48.380
-[Brady] You've never tasted Coca Cola?
-I've never tasted Coca Cola.

00:00:48.400 --> 00:00:50.200
-One little sip?
-Not a sip.

00:00:50.440 --> 00:00:54.340
-Are you allergic to it, or...? 
-No, no, it just doesn't appeal to me.

00:00:54.460 --> 00:00:58.480
And now I've got to this age, 
I don't want to spoil my record.

00:00:59.000 --> 00:01:02.660
I might be the oldest non-Coke drinker in the world!

00:01:02.860 --> 00:01:04.620
[Brady laughs]

00:01:04.620 --> 00:01:06.260
There's not coke in there, is there?

00:01:06.880 --> 00:01:07.860
No.

00:01:08.100 --> 00:01:12.920
Then I persuaded Neil that it was worth doing.

00:01:14.720 --> 00:01:17.420
[fizzling]

00:01:19.400 --> 00:01:25.720
As you can expect, when the can was dropped in, the nitrogen started boiling, a lot.

00:01:27.040 --> 00:01:33.140
What I was interested in is that CO₂ is quite soluble in cold liquid,

00:01:33.149 --> 00:01:37.270
but it's not soluble in ice, or it's poorly soluble.

00:01:37.640 --> 00:01:42.940
So, of course, what should happen is that when the Coke freezes,

00:01:43.200 --> 00:01:48.179
the CO₂ should come out, as a gas, 
and the pressure should go up.

00:01:48.700 --> 00:01:56.820
At the same time, because ice is less dense than liquid, the liquid in the Coke can should expand.

00:01:56.969 --> 00:02:02.929
But because liquid nitrogen is pretty cold, the CO₂ may well solidify,

00:02:03.000 --> 00:02:06.040
so the effect will be less than if it was a gas.

00:02:06.440 --> 00:02:13.420
So what I was suspecting is that the can would break... I hoped, quite spectacularly.

00:02:22.760 --> 00:02:34.060
And the first time we did it was quite exciting, because the can split before all the Coke had frozen.

00:02:34.090 --> 00:02:40.170
A slit, almost as if it had been cut by a knife, appeared in the side of the can,

00:02:40.340 --> 00:02:42.700
and the Coke... came out.

00:02:42.960 --> 00:02:48.860
And, of course, as soon as the liquid, which, Coke is mostly water, and some sugar,

00:02:49.460 --> 00:02:54.560
as soon as it came in contact with the liquid nitrogen, it froze.

00:02:55.300 --> 00:02:58.940
But there was much bubbling, so it froze into a sort of froth.

00:02:59.240 --> 00:03:00.900
A bit like a water-ice.

00:03:05.360 --> 00:03:09.360
In the end, what came out looked like an artwork!

00:03:10.340 --> 00:03:17.420
And I persuaded great dear Neil that we should watch it melting.

00:03:17.720 --> 00:03:20.820
And I got quite excited going back and looking at it!

00:03:20.940 --> 00:03:24.460
[Brady] The professor was more excited by the melting than anything we did all day.

00:03:24.470 --> 00:03:31.550
[Dr. Poliakoff] The second can, which, we did rather better with- better lighting, things like that -

00:03:32.000 --> 00:03:35.200
was very disappointing.

00:03:45.260 --> 00:03:51.300
Because it froze completely before the can split.

00:03:56.240 --> 00:04:01.100
And the can split again, but everything inside was solid, so nothing came out.

00:04:01.100 --> 00:04:04.260
What was quite interesting is that when you look through the slit,

00:04:04.260 --> 00:04:08.720
the ice, instead of being a solid lump, was in much smaller pieces.

00:04:09.740 --> 00:04:12.700
[ice cracking]

00:04:14.180 --> 00:04:17.040
Not quite like sand, but quite powdery.

00:04:17.300 --> 00:04:20.540
That didn't surprise me, 
because I've known for many years

00:04:20.540 --> 00:04:24.400
that if you put ice in liquid nitrogen, it does crack.

00:04:28.740 --> 00:04:36.240
Finally, Brady thought we ought to be fair, and we ought to do Pepsi, as well as Coke.

00:04:36.410 --> 00:04:39.530
-[Brady] Have you tried Pepsi?
-I haven't tried Pepsi, either.

00:04:39.560 --> 00:04:42.860
-Okay.
-I include them as the same sort of thing.

00:04:42.860 --> 00:04:48.520
Unfortunately, the machine we've got only sells bottles of Pepsi.

00:04:48.540 --> 00:04:55.340
So freezing a plastic bottle of Pepsi, we expected it to have split in much the same way.

00:05:00.800 --> 00:05:03.260
[hissing]

00:05:04.490 --> 00:05:14.590
But in fact, it was very disappointing, because the bottle froze completely without splitting at all.

00:05:14.800 --> 00:05:17.380
I got bored and left.

00:05:22.860 --> 00:05:25.440
[ice cracking]

00:05:31.760 --> 00:05:38.320
Brady and Neil couldn't restrain themselves from tasting the frozen Pepsi, but I kept my pledge.

00:05:39.560 --> 00:05:44.360
It's nice and warm, but I suppose gold color
also gives you a feeling of warmth,

00:05:44.360 --> 00:05:46.740
so it may be partly psychological.

00:05:48.760 --> 00:05:54.360
The reason why the bank has got this store, 
is because not only the Bank of England,

00:05:54.420 --> 00:05:59.640
but other central banks, 
like to keep some of their money reserves in gold.

